      "readme": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "display": {
            "name": "README"
          },
          "plugin": {
            "kind": "Markdown",
            "spec": {
              "text": "# Thalamus Dev Dashboard\n\nObservability for vLLM deployments in the `thalamus` namespace.\n\n## Sections\n\n1. **Requests** — Success rate, error rate, running and waiting requests. All grouped by `model_name`.\n2. **Throughput** — Prompt and generation tokens/sec (row 1, per `model_name`); replica count per deployment and KV cache usage per model (row 2).\n3. **Latency** — TTFT and TPOT, p50 and p99 in separate panels (to keep series count manageable when grouped by model).\n4. **GPU & Startup** — GPU utilization timeseries (per-pod via DCGM), aggregate GPU stat, and pod startup time, side by side.\n5. **README** — this section.\n\n## Variables\n\n- `$rate_interval` — smoothing window for request counters and token throughput rates. Increase under low/sparse traffic.\n- `$latency_interval` — smoothing window for TTFT/TPOT histograms. Tuned independently from rate-interval since latency and traffic shape have different noise characteristics.\n\nDefaults: `1h`. Drop to `5m` or `1m` once steady traffic exists.\n\n## Metric notes\n\n- **Error rate** uses `vllm:request_success_total{finished_reason=~\"abort|error\"}` rather than `vllm:request_failure_total` — the latter doesn't exist on the vLLM version we run.\n- **TPOT** uses `vllm:inter_token_latency_seconds_bucket`. The older `vllm:time_per_output_token_seconds_bucket` metric is from an earlier vLLM version.\n- **GPU utilization** is a device-level DCGM metric. The `exported_pod` label maps GPU → pod, and since one pod hosts one model, this is effectively per-model. Stat aggregates across all GPUs.\n- **Time to ready** uses `kube_pod_status_ready_time - kube_pod_created`. Both metrics may not be emitted by the current `kube-state-metrics` version — chart will be empty until they appear.\n- **KV cache** sits in the Throughput section since it correlates directly with concurrent request load.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Verify `model_name` label exists on all vLLM metrics under the current chart version. If a metric uses a different label name (e.g., `model`), its panel will collapse to a single unlabeled series — that's the signal to fix.\n- Once `kube_pod_status_ready_time` becomes available, validate the startup query end-to-end.\n"
            }
          }
        }
      }
